# Native process memory: Fai / Rust / OCaml

**Snapshot:** `fixture-checkpoint` · **Completed:** 2026-10-12T00:00:01Z

**Measured main revision:** [`aaaaaaaaaaaa`](https://github.com/example/fai/tree/aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa) · **Method:** `isolated-aot-v2`

**Host:** fixture / fixture · **CPU:** fixture

**Peers:** fixture · OCaml fixture (Flambda, `-O3`)

[Raw data, identities and reproducibility record](https://example.test/evidence)

## Current coverage

**40 workloads · 160 size/repetition cases · 5 fresh processes per side and mode.** Both registered input sizes and 1/16 whole-workload repetitions are included. Every peer is an isolated, uninstrumented executable.

The tables report **process footprint**, including code, stacks, allocator retention and GC policy. Each displayed peak and floor is the maximum across fresh processes, in KiB (1,024 bytes). They do not measure comparable cross-language live heaps. Floors use the same executable; peaks are never subtracted or converted into compiler-memory win counts.

## Status legend

The floor is the same executable running its harness without the workload. `Process F/R` compares Fai's peak RSS with Rust's; `Process F/O` compares it with OCaml's. Lower ratios mean a smaller observed process footprint.

| Status | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `application/unqualified` | Peers differ in algorithms, containers or implementation policies. Absolute RSS is shown; both ratios are withheld. |
| `floor-limited` | At least one peer's floor reaches 10% of its workload peak RSS. Both memory ratios are withheld. |
| `process footprint only` | The reviewed matched kernel uses uninstrumented peers, at least five observations per peer, and every peer's floor is below 10% of its peak. Ratios qualify as whole-process RSS comparisons. |

`—` means a ratio was excluded by these rules; the underlying measurements remain in the table and evidence. Whole-process RSS includes code, stacks, allocator retention and GC policy. Live-heap efficiency and Perceus guarantees require their own evidence.
